# Lean loop (efficient + good)

**Bar:** good enough to ship correctly - not exhaustive planning or verification theater.

Do it **well** (right product, working code, no CRITICAL/HIGH left when reviews ran). Do it **efficiently** (shortest path: light plan, minimal checks, **skip reviews often**, no redo of work already done).

## Anti-overkill (always)

- **Do not** over-plan - specs/rubrics only as deep as size needs; no essays.
- **Do not** over-check - run the gates that matter for *this* change; skip unrelated suites.
- **Do not** launch heavy `code-reviewer` every time - see [reviewer-defaults](reviewer-defaults.mdc).
- **Do not** re-research / re-audit the whole repo when the touch set is clear.
- **Do not** add “nice to have” reviewers, docs, or ADRs unless the change requires them.
- **One pass** of research → decide → act. Prefer shipping a correct small slice over polishing the process.

## Tracks

| Track | Behavior |
|-|--|
| **Direct** | Implement immediately on `development`. No planner, no mandatory GitHub issue, **no reviewers**. |
| **Feature** | Right-sized harness; **review often skipped** for micro-S/S; light when M+. |

## Auto-continue (do not ask)

On **Feature** track, once discuss-gate is PASS (or skipped with stated assumptions) and size is decided, **continue without asking**:

```text
planner → [designer only if design-when - HTML] → generator → [reviewers only if needed] → fix if blocked → doc-updater if needed → changelog version+date → **commit + push development (Refs #n)** → GitHub evidence comment
```

On **Direct** track: research → implement → verify. Do not wait for the human between steps.

**Never ask** before: launching planner/generator/reviewers, starting the next fix round, consolidating PASS/BLOCK, or continuing Direct work.

**Pause only when** the human must act:

| Pause | Why |
|-|-|
| Product question that **blocks** correct Feature work | Missing Visual / API / Content / Data that cannot be assumed |
| ADR acceptance | Architectural fork |
| Stop-early | Security CRITICAL or wrong-intent frontend FAIL on i1 |
| Generator round 3 exhausted | Human triage |
| External blocker | Secrets, credentials, env the agent cannot obtain |

Do **not** pause for: process, size, reviewers, design/wireframe approval, “OK to implement?”, “shall I continue?”, or testing-gate sign-off theater.

One-line status updates are fine. Prefer **doing** over waiting. Designer is **not** the default for S-M UI - see [design-when](design-when.mdc).

## Lean reviews (when they run)

Bias: **skip**. When a reviewer *does* run, keep it **diff-scoped and short**.

### Scope

- Read **only** the files / hunks this issue changed (plus AC checklist skim).
- Do **not** re-audit the whole repo or paste large code blocks.

### Output budget (hard)

Feedback ≤~25-40 lines. Prefer:

```markdown
# <type> - <id> i<n>
## Verdict: PASS | BLOCK
## Gates (one line each)
- typecheck: pass
## Blockers (CRITICAL/HIGH only)
- `path:line` - problem → fix
## Notes (optional, ≤2 bullets, or omit)
```

Rules:

- Empty CRITICAL/HIGH → omit or `None`.
- No long MEDIUM/LOW catalogs.
- Prefer `file:line` + one-sentence fix.

### Parent Task prompts

Short: issue id, iteration, paths, “lean review”, output path. Prefer faster models unless auth/crypto.

### Reviewer set

See [reviewer-defaults](reviewer-defaults.mdc). **Bias to skip.**

- Direct / micro-S / docs-only → **no reviewers**
- S Feature → usually **no** `code-reviewer`; specialists only if API/auth/UI/DB touched
- M+ → light `code-reviewer` **or** one specialist - not both for the same concern
- Never add “nice to have” reviewers

## Efficient + good

| Do | Don't |
|--|-|
| Light Feature plan that encodes testable AC | Multi-page specs for S work |
| Skip or light reviews | Heavy `code-reviewer` every time |
| Targeted `setup.md` commands for touched surfaces | Re-run every gate on a one-line fix |
| TDD on changed behavior | Speculative tests for untouched paths |
| Fix CRITICAL/HIGH only when a review ran | Endless MEDIUM/LOW polish loops |

Direct: fix fast with a regression test when behavior changes.
